Subject: Wellma – Points Raised with L Catterton
Hi all,
Please find below a summary of the key points we raised in our discussion with L Catterton on the Wellma process.
Overall Stance
	•	Aligned on the continued growth and the fact the business has executed well in a number of areas.
	•	More cautious on the marketed EBITDA and certain elements of the operating and growth profile that require further diligence.
Key Points Raised
EBITDA and ramp rates
	•	Skeptical of the EBITDA being marketed for the take-private.
	•	Some of the ramp-rate adjustments look aggressive and need to be better understood.
	•	Appears to be upside in the rest-of-year performance relative to what is currently reflected, which could help offset other areas of concern.
Site-level performance
	•	Performance is uneven across the footprint.
	•	Some sites have performed very well; others (including Barcelona, the UK and Italy) are not growing or are declining.
	•	Management has attributed this in part to customer losses, but greater comfort is needed given the complexity of the operational footprint being acquired.
	•	This is not a venture-style situation where underperforming sites can simply be written off.
Pipeline quality
	•	Clearer view needed of what is actually in the pipeline.
	•	Specifically, what is truly strategic, a good fit, and actionable versus more opportunistic.
	•	Understanding where the real opportunities sit is important to underwriting the growth.
Cross-sell and format thesis
	•	Most customers currently buy a single SKU.
	•	Core thesis is that customers will buy across formats, but the evidence of this happening at scale is not yet as tangible as we would like.
	•	Need more conviction on the multi-format opportunity.
Customer and product mix
	•	Business has been growing in lower-margin, higher-volume commodity categories (including certain titles and sports).
	•	Raises a positioning question: is this a differentiated technology platform, or more of a diversified platform with a mix of unique and commoditised offerings?
	•	The two command different valuations.
	•	Caps continue to represent a large share of the business (and grow as a percentage of the plan outside North America); need comfort on the risk of being competed away on price in these areas.
Capacity and investment to support growth
	•	Questioned whether sufficient operational investment is reflected in the plan to support the projected growth.
	•	Some of the margin uplift from operational leverage may assume a lighter investment profile than may ultimately be required.
Valuation and process positioning
	•	Discussed whether buyers will underwrite on the full ~€60m or a lower figure (e.g. mid-50s) to reflect ramp-rate risk.
	•	Sense that expectations have moderated versus the initial “high-growth, high-margin” marketing, and that the process feels somewhat weaker than it did earlier.
	•	Even under conservative assumptions (35% IRR with multiple contraction), models still support valuations in the €800m+ range.
	•	One option is to indicate a multiple without locking in a specific profit number at this stage.
Happy to discuss any of the above.
